{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t03-013",
  "track": "track-03",
  "title": "performance troubleshooting monitoring waits and query tuning",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Troubleshooting begins with a measurable symptom, affected workload, time window, and recent change history; 'slow database' is not yet a diagnosis.",
    "Waits and resource metrics show where work is queued, while query-level evidence identifies expensive statements. A wait category alone does not prove a root cause.",
    "Establish a baseline, compare actual versus expected resource use, and validate with representative workload. Fixing CPU, I/O, memory, blocking, or application-pool pressure requires different interventions.",
    "Use Query Store, DMVs, and Extended Events to collect bounded evidence; minimize diagnostic overhead and protect sensitive query data.",
    "Tune iteratively and keep a rollback path. A local improvement can regress concurrency, write cost, cache behavior, or tail latency elsewhere."
  ],
