  "accuracyFacts": [
    "sys.dm_os_wait_stats contains completed waits accumulated since engine startup or the last explicit reset; it does not show current waits, query-level attribution, or persisted history. Capture deltas across a known interval and account for restart/reset boundaries.",
    "SQL Server 2022 and later generally require VIEW SERVER PERFORMANCE STATE for server-scoped performance DMVs; exact permissions can differ by DMV and database-scoped views. Do not recommend granting sysadmin for diagnostics.",
    "A wait type is a clue about a queue or stalled resource, not a root-cause verdict. PAGEIOLATCH concerns waiting for data pages read into the buffer pool; PAGELATCH is an in-memory latch. WRITELOG is log flush waiting; SOS_SCHEDULER_YIELD can signal runnable CPU pressure but needs corroboration; CXPACKET and CXCONSUMER must be interpreted with plan shape and skew.",
    "Parallel wait labels changed across releases: starting with SQL Server 2022, CXPACKET refers to producer-thread waits, CXCONSUMER tracks consumer-thread waits, and exchange synchronization has CXSYNC_PORT/CXSYNC_CONSUMER categories. Do not compare raw wait-type names across releases as if their categorization were unchanged.",
    "Query Store retains query, plan, and aggregated runtime history in time intervals, unlike the transient plan cache. Data capture can become read-only when storage limits or errors are reached. Plan forcing asks the optimizer to use a recorded plan; forcing can fail and emit an event, and forcing must have an explicit verification and removal path.",
    "SQL Server Query Store is enabled by default for new databases starting in SQL Server 2022; existing databases and earlier releases can differ. Query Store for readable secondary replicas is available starting in SQL Server 2025 but is not enabled by default.",
    "DMV counters have different lifetimes and scopes. For example, query execution statistics describe cached plans and can disappear on eviction/restart; I/O and index usage counters are not durable workload history. Record timestamps, database/instance identity, and counter resets when comparing snapshots.",
    "Missing-index DMVs are optimizer suggestions, not a complete index design or priority ranking. They omit write, storage, overlap, filtered-index, and workload-wide tradeoffs; validate a candidate against the representative workload and existing indexes.",
    "Do not treat a single wait threshold, average latency, or server-wide counter as proof. Correlate the affected query/workload and time window with query-level evidence, plans, file latency, concurrency, and application telemetry.",
    "SQL Server 2025 CE feedback for expressions supports compatibility level 160 and later, OPPO requires compatibility level 170, and OPTIMIZED_SP_EXECUTESQL is a separate configuration. Verify documented eligibility before attributing a result to an IQP feature.",
    "The documented SQL Server 2022+ permissions for server-scoped performance DMVs use VIEW SERVER PERFORMANCE STATE, subject to per-object exceptions and database-scoped permissions."
  ]
}
